From 9b7c6da79198b2b4e8543e0fc1ef5ee05bb46ad4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sarin <me@s4r.in>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 10:56:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Convert captures of HDR outputs to SDR

A capture of an HDR output holds PQ-encoded BT.2020 samples. Written to a
PNG as-is those are interpreted as ordinary SDR values, which is why such
a screenshot looks wrong in every viewer that does not do color
management, i.e. nearly all of them.

ext-image-copy-capture does not say what encoding a capture is in, but
wp_color_manager_v1 answers that for an output directly: get_output gives
an image description whose information includes the named transfer
function, the named primaries and the reference white luminance.

Use it to detect a PQ-encoded capture and convert it: decode PQ to
absolute luminance, divide by the reference white so ordinary SDR content
lands at 1.0, convert BT.2020 to BT.709 primaries and encode with a 2.2
power curve. Highlights above the reference white cannot be represented
and are clipped.

The 2.2 curve rather than the piecewise sRGB one is what compositors use
to encode SDR output; measured against a capture of the same content with
HDR turned off, it brings the difference down from 1.4% to 0.6% RMSE,
which is within the noise of two captures taken a second apart.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
